WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
The central rule of this course is to trust file objects, not just paths.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.880
Paths are input.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.980
Directories are trust boundaries.

4
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:19.200
The object actually opened or created is what the program should reason about when it reads, writes, replaces, traverses, or cleans up.

5
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.320
Use practical review questions.

6
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.420
Where did this path come from?

7
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.120
Is the directory trusted?

8
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.940
Can another actor modify entries there?

9
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.400
Is the operation based on a path string or an opened object?

10
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.820
Is link-following behavior intentional?

11
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.960
Are permissions and ownership checked appropriately?

12
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:42.760
Creation, replacement, temporary files, and cleanup need the same level of care.

13
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:54.620
Create files safely, avoid repeated ambiguous path lookups, validate opened objects where possible, separate working files from final output, and document what traversal code does with links.

14
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.820
Finally, keep the policy alive through tests and review.

15
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:07.560
Exercise directory trust assumptions, link policy, replacement behavior, cleanup behavior, permissions, and failure paths.

16
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:14.780
Symbolic-link safety comes from platform-aware contracts and maintainable file-handling design, not from one isolated function call.
